.TH FSCK.FAT 8
.SH NAME
fsck.fat \- check and repair FAT16 and FAT32 filesystems
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B fsck.fat
[
.B \-fnpvy
]
.I filesystem
[
.I filesystem ...
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B fsck.fat
checks FAT copies, cluster chains, directories, lost clusters, and FAT32
FSInfo metadata.
The filesystem must not be mounted read-write while it is checked.
.PP
The checker uses a compact allocation bitmap and reads the FAT a sector at a
time, so its memory use is suitable for small ReBSD systems.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-n
Open the filesystem read-only and answer no to all repair questions.
.TP
.B \-y
Answer yes to all repair questions.
.TP
.B \-p
Preen mode.  Apply repairs marked safe and leave destructive choices
unresolved.
.TP
.B \-f
Force a full check.  A full check is currently always performed.
.TP
.B \-v
Print additional volume information.
.SH EXIT STATUS
Zero means the filesystem was clean.
One means errors were corrected.
Four means errors remain uncorrected.
Eight means an operational error prevented the check.
.SH SEE ALSO
fsck(8), fsck.ufs(8), mount(8)
